<p>An agent that keeps working after you close your laptop has solved a scheduling problem. It hasn&rsquo;t necessarily solved an autonomy problem. OpenAI&rsquo;s new product, dots, is a good place to see the difference, because its own documentation draws the line more honestly than its launch language does.</p>

<p>Here&rsquo;s what&rsquo;s on the record. At its developer conference (DevDay) at the end of September, OpenAI introduced <a href="https://openai.com/index/introducing-dots/">dots</a>, which it describes as &ldquo;remarkably capable, always-on agents.&rdquo; Each one runs on Generative Pre-trained Transformer 6 (GPT-6) Astra, the model OpenAI says powers the product, with its own cloud computer, its own browser, and access to more than 4,000 apps. A dot can &ldquo;take a project and run with it, even while it&rsquo;s working on several others.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s the pitch. I haven&rsquo;t run one, so everything below is a reading of OpenAI&rsquo;s published pages, not a test. My read is that the interesting part isn&rsquo;t the pitch; it&rsquo;s the settings page.</p>

<h2>What&rsquo;s the dial?</h2>

<p>OpenAI&rsquo;s <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001530-getting-started-with-your-dot">help article</a> lets you set a rule for each kind of action, and the rule has three positions: &ldquo;Take action without asking,&rdquo; &ldquo;Take action if pre-approved,&rdquo; or &ldquo;Ask before taking action.&rdquo; Pre-approved means you asked for that exact action in your prompt. Some jobs, like changing a password, always stay with the human, no matter what the dial says.</p>

<p>I like this design. It treats approval as a setting you choose per action rather than a property baked into the product, which is how handoffs should work. It also tells you something the marketing doesn&rsquo;t: the product ships with a position on the dial called &ldquo;ask,&rdquo; and OpenAI expects people to use it.</p>

<h2>Where does it land?</h2>

<p>Under the <a href="/evaluation">Evaluation framework</a>, a handoff isn&rsquo;t a defect. A system that stops for a human at every consequential step, though, is being operated at a lower level than the capability it might have. So a dot isn&rsquo;t one level; it&rsquo;s whatever level you dial it to. Set every action to &ldquo;ask&rdquo; and you&rsquo;ve built a well-staffed assistant. Set most of them to &ldquo;act&rdquo; and you&rsquo;re closer to the threshold the <a href="/maturity">Maturity Model</a> describes. My forecast is that most people will leave most of it on &ldquo;ask&rdquo; for months, because the cost of one wrong email to a client is higher than the cost of a hundred approval clicks.</p>

<p>Here&rsquo;s the part the dial can&rsquo;t fix. OpenAI&rsquo;s own page says &ldquo;Dots can still make mistakes, so always review consequential work,&rdquo; and the help article repeats it, including mistakes made &ldquo;when following your rules.&rdquo; Read that as a capability statement. The dot can do the work; the human still decides whether the work is done and whether it&rsquo;s right. Deciding that a task is complete is one of the capabilities the framework requires of the system itself, and it&rsquo;s the one this product hands back to you by design.</p>

<h2>Is always-on the same as continuous?</h2>

<p>No, and I made this argument at length in <a href="/blog/duration-is-not-autonomy">Duration is not autonomy</a>. Running while you sleep measures how long one process stays alive. Continuity asks whether the goal survives that process ending: a crash, a rate limit, a dead run. OpenAI says a dot carries context across channels and keeps monitoring in the background with read-only tools, which sounds like state that outlives a single run. The documentation doesn&rsquo;t say what happens when a run fails partway, or how a dot notices that it did. Those are the questions I&rsquo;d put to it first, because this site has spent two months documenting what happens when nobody asks them (see <a href="/blog/record-cant-be-its-own-receipt">A record can&rsquo;t be its own receipt</a>).</p>

<p>The fair counterargument is that a product launch page isn&rsquo;t a specification, and OpenAI may well have answers it simply didn&rsquo;t publish. That&rsquo;s true. It&rsquo;s also true that the activity view, where you can &ldquo;open your dot&rsquo;s computer at any time to inspect its work,&rdquo; is the company&rsquo;s own admission that inspection by a person is part of the loop.</p>

<p>So here&rsquo;s the test I&rsquo;d run, and you can run it too. Give a dot a task with a deadline, kill its connection halfway through, and see who notices first. If the answer is you, you bought persistence. If the answer is the dot, you bought something closer to the standard. I&rsquo;d love to see which.</p>

